@question {{id}} | {{title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{objective}}
@given {{given}}
@domain L1, L2 and L3 form a student attempt containing an error; later lines may carry an earlier error. The determinant rule is ad-bc.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which line first uses the determinant rule incorrectly?
{{choices_1}}
@feedback {{l2_id}} | L2 only evaluates the plus expression already written in L1; L1 is where subtraction first becomes addition.
@feedback {{l3_id}} | L3 carries the earlier determinant error; the first sign error is in L1.
@after {{after_1}}
@wrong Locate the first place where subtraction became addition.
@why The first error replaces the required subtraction with addition; later lines may carry that error. L_1 uses {{sum_expression}}; the rule requires {{determinant_expression}}.
@step 20 | What is the corrected determinant?
{{choices_2}}
@feedback {{sum_id}} | This adds the diagonal products: {{sum_expression}}={{sum_value}}. The determinant subtracts the second product, giving {{determinant}}, not {{sum_label}}.
@feedback {{other_id}} | This omits the other diagonal product after ({{a}})({{d}})={{main_product}}. Subtract ({{b}})({{c}})={{other_product}} to obtain {{determinant}}, not {{other_label}}.
@after {{after_2}}
@wrong Recompute ad-bc from the original four entries.
@why Now use the required subtraction on the original entries: {{determinant_expression}}={{determinant}}.
@end
